{"decisive_point":"The claimed quantitative law for obstruction prevalence is false. Stable mixed configurations are exactly cuts whose crossing edges form a matching. For a fixed cut with fraction a of vertices in one color, the probability that every vertex has at most one crossing neighbor is asymptotically exp(...). Multiplying by the number of such cuts gives exponent f(a)... whose maximum over a in (0,1) is 0 at a=1/2. Therefore the expected number of stable mixed configurations is O(1), not Theta(exp(c n)) with c approx 0.07-0.1.","verdict":"fail"}